# The Markdown dialect (§6.4)

This module is the specification and the implementation of the Markdown prose
dialect. The prose is the specification. The chunks after it are the
implementation.

## Why Markdown {#sec:xZ8V}

The ergonomic prose dialect is CommonMark, not LaTeX. In `html` mode the author
wraps every paragraph in a `<p>` and every display equation in an equation div,
and that is the main keystroke cost of the dialect (§18 R5). Markdown removes
that cost and keeps the pipeline shape the same. Markdown also replaces the
LaTeX text expander the first plan called for: it is more ergonomic than raw
HTML and lets lit drop the hardest part of that plan. Only the math-only macro
table stays, as roadmap (§6.5).

## The transform {#sec:TAUn}

lit renders CommonMark with `pulldown-cmark`, as a post-`markup` transform
(§6.2). It runs on the reassembled prose of a docs chunk, between step 2 and the
HTML rewrite, and only in `markdown` mode. GFM tables are on only inside a
`table` fence, never in bare prose, so a stray `|` stays literal. The order is:

1. Protect fenced blocks. The fence info line is a kind and an optional
   `\label{ns:name}`. lit knows three directives: `math`, `figure`, and `table`.
   Any other info is an ordinary code fence, stashed so step 3 never scans it for
   a `$`. lit renders each float now. It emits a `math` float in place; it stashes
   a `figure` or `table` float behind a placeholder, because their bodies are
   multi-line HTML the outer CommonMark must not reflow.
   - A `math` fence is display-math sugar. Its body is bare LaTeX. The fence is
     the delimiter, so a `$` is redundant and never scanned. lit emits an
     equation div.
   - A `figure` fence takes an optional leading brace-balanced `\caption{…}`,
     rendered as inline Markdown and math, then a block-Markdown body, and emits
     a `<figure>` with an optional `<figcaption>`.
   - A `table` fence takes an optional `\caption{…}`, then a GFM pipe table. lit
     splices the id and a `<caption>` into the `<table>` that pulldown-cmark
     emits.
2. Lower `\ref{ns:name}` to the same `<a data-ref>` the `html` dialect and the
   resolver (§7) already handle, so a fenced reference and a hand-written one are
   identical. This runs on raw prose and skips a `<code>` or `<pre>`. A `\ref`
   inside a backtick span is a documented no-op; write it in `html` mode.
3. Extract math (§6.2 step 2) on the remaining prose. Float-derived math indices
   come first, inline math after; both resolve at render.
4. Render CommonMark to HTML. A backtick span and a reassembled inline quote both
   become a `<code>`. Math placeholders, stashed floats, and raw-HTML islands
   pass through. lit then swaps the stashed floats back in.

## Labels and namespaces {#sec:k8T0}

A `\label{ns:name}` namespace must match the fence kind: `eq:` for `math`,
`fig:` for `figure`, `tbl:` for `table`. The rewrite pass keys a label off its
id prefix (§7), so a mismatched namespace would be dropped in silence. lit
catches it as E030 at render time. A float with no `\label` gets no id and is not
referenceable, because an unreferenceable float has no stable identity (§7.1). A
fenced float emits exactly the same tagged element an island would, so all
downstream identity and resolution is the same as `html` mode.

## Raw-HTML islands {#sec:UWqg}

CommonMark passes block-level raw HTML through untouched. So anything a directive
does not cover, most often a sidenote aside, is written as raw HTML dropped
inside the Markdown. `examples/ring.nw` is the worked example. `fixtures/md.nw`
is the end-to-end fixture that exercises all three directives and `\ref`.

## How the code implements this {#sec:lisV}

The module is the error type, the top-level render, the shared prose path, the
raw helpers that skip code and balance braces, the fence machinery, the
per-directive renderers, and the restore pass.
